'''''Turritopsis dohrnii''''', commonly known as the '''immortal jellyfish''', is a [[species]] of small, [[Biological immortality |biologically immortal]] [[jellyfish|Medusozoa]] (''[[Jellyfish#Names|jellyfish]]'')<ref>{{cite journal|last=Bavestrello|first=Giorgio |author2=Christian Sommer |author3=Michele Sarà|year=1992|title=Bi-directional conversion in Turritopsis nutricula (Hydrozoa)|journal=[[Scientia Marina]]|volume=56|issue=2–3|pages=137–140}}</ref><ref name="piraino-96">{{cite journal|last=Piraino|first=Stefano|author2=F. Boero |author3=B. Aeschbach |author4=V. Schmid |year=1996|title=Reversing the life cycle: medusae transforming into polyps and cell transdifferentiation in Turritopsis nutricula (Cnidaria, Hydrozoa)|journal=[[Biological Bulletin]]|volume=190|issue=3|pages=302–312|doi=10.2307/1543022|pmid=29227703|jstor=1543022}}</ref> found worldwide in [[Temperate climate|temperate]] to [[Tropical climate|tropic]] waters. It is one of the few known cases of an animal capable of completely reverting to a sexually immature, sedentary polyps [[Jellyfish#Life cycle|colonial stage]] after having reached sexual maturity as a solitary individual.

Like most other [[hydrozoan]]s, ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' begin their lives as tiny, free-swimming [[larva]]e known as [[planula]]e. As a planula settles down, it gives rise to a colony of [[polyp (zoology)|polyps]] that are attached to the [[Seabed|sea floor]]. All the polyps and [[medusa (biology)|medusa]] arising from a single planula are [[Cloning#Natural_cloning|clones]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Jellyfish and Comb Jellies {{!}} Smithsonian Ocean|url=http://ocean.si.edu/ocean-life/invertebrates/jellyfish-and-comb-jellies|website=[[Smithsonian Institute|si.edu]]|access-date=2020-10-19|date=30 April 2018 |language=en}}</ref> The polyps form into an extensively branched form, which is not commonly seen in most jellyfish. Jellyfish, also known as medusae, then bud off these polyps and continue their life in a free-swimming form, eventually becoming sexually mature. When sexually mature, they are known to prey on other jellyfish species at a rapid pace. If the ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' jellyfish is exposed to environmental stress, physical assault, or is sick or old, it can revert to the polyp stage, forming a new polyp colony.<ref name=nytm/> It does this through the cell development process of [[transdifferentiation]], which alters the [[cellular differentiation|differentiated state of the cells]] and transforms them into new types of cells.

Theoretically, this process can go on indefinitely, effectively rendering the jellyfish biologically immortal,<ref name="piraino-96"/><ref name="CheatingDeath">{{cite web|url=http://8e.devbio.com/preview_article.php?ch=2&id=6|title=Cheating Death: The Immortal Life Cycle of Turritopsis|last=Gilbert|first=Scott F.|year=2006|access-date=2009-03-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100402085711/http://8e.devbio.com/preview_article.php?ch=2&id=6|archive-date=2010-04-02}}</ref> although in practice individuals can still die. In nature, most ''Turritopsis'' ''dohrnii'' are likely to succumb to predation or disease in the medusa stage without reverting to the polyp form.<ref name="National Geographic News">{{cite web
|title="Immortal" Jellyfish Swarm World's Oceans
|author=Ker Than
|url=http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2009/01/090130-immortal-jellyfish-swarm.html
|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090202063305/http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2009/01/090130-immortal-jellyfish-swarm.html
|archive-date=February 2, 2009
|date=January 29, 2009
|publisher=[[National Geographic News]]
|access-date=2010-06-16
}}</ref>

The capability of biological immortality with no maximum lifespan makes ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' an important target of basic biological [[Ageing|aging]] and [[Pharmaceutical drug|pharmaceutical]] research.<ref name=":0" />

==Taxonomy==

The species was formerly considered [[Biological_specificity#Conspecific|conspecific]] with ''[[Turritopsis nutricula|T.&nbsp;nutricula]]'' before being reclassified as a separate species.<ref name=mig/> It was named in 1883 in honour of [[Anton Dohrn]], the founder of the [[Stazione Zoologica Anton Dohrn]] in [[Naples]], [[Italy]].<ref>{{cite book|last=Weismann|first=A.|year=1883|title=Die Entstehung der Sexualzellen bei den Hydromedusen. Zugleich ein Beitrag zur Kenntnis des Baues und der Lebenserscheinungen dieser Gruppe|publisher=Gustav Fischer|location= Jena| pages= |url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/9646925| language= de}}</ref>

Until a 2006 study, it was thought that ''[[Turritopsis rubra]]'' and ''[[Turritopsis nutricula]]'' were the same species as ''Turritopsis dohrnii''.<ref name=mig/> It is not known whether or not ''T.&nbsp;rubra'' medusae can also transform back into polyps.

== Description ==

The medusa of ''Turritopsis dohrnii'' is bell-shaped made of [[mesoglea]], with a maximum diameter of about {{convert|4.5|mm|in}} and is about as tall as it is wide.<ref>{{cite journal|last=Kramp|first=P. L.|title=Synopsis of the medusae of the world|journal=Journal of the Marine Biological Association of the United Kingdom|year=1961|volume=40|pages=1–469|doi=10.1017/s0025315400007347|bibcode=1961JMBUK..40....7K }}</ref><ref name="TimesOnline26012009" /> The [[mesoglea]] in the walls of the bell is uniformly thin, except for some thickening at the apex. The relatively large stomach is bright red and has a cruciform shape in cross section. Young specimens 1&nbsp;mm in diameter have only eight tentacles evenly spaced out along the edge,<ref name=":1">{{Cite journal|last1=Martell|first1=L.|last2=Piraino|first2=S.|last3=Gravili|first3=C.|last4=Boero|first4=F.|date=2016-07-02|title=Life cycle, morphology and medusa ontogenesis of Turritopsis dohrnii (Cnidaria: Hydrozoa)|journal=Italian Journal of Zoology|volume=83|issue=3|pages=390–399|doi=10.1080/11250003.2016.1203034|issn=1125-0003|doi-access=free}}</ref> whereas adult specimens have 80–90 tentacles.{{Citation needed|date=May 2023}} The medusa (jellyfish) is free-living in the [[plankton]]. Dense [[nerve net]] cells are also present in the [[epidermis]] in the cap. They form a large ring-like structure above the radial canal commonly presented in [[Cnidaria|cnidarians]].<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Koizumi|first1=Osamu|last2=Hamada|first2=Shun|last3=Minobe|first3=Sumiko|last4=Hamaguchi-Hamada|first4=Kayoko|last5=Kurumata-Shigeto|first5=Mami|last6=Nakamura|first6=Masaru|last7=Namikawa|first7=Hiroshi|title=The nerve ring in cnidarians: its presence and structure in hydrozoan medusae|journal=Zoology|volume=118|issue=2|pages=79–88|doi=10.1016/j.zool.2014.10.001|pmid=25498132|year=2015|bibcode=2015Zool..118...79K }}</ref>

''Turritopsis dohrnii'' also has a bottom-living polyp form, or hydroid, which consists of [[stolon]]s that run along the substrate and upright branches with feeding polyps that can produce medusa buds.<ref>{{cite book|last=Fraser|first=C. McLean|title=Hydroids of the Pacific Coast of Canada and the United States|publisher=University of Toronto Press|year=1937|pages=201 plus 44 plates}}</ref> These polyps develop over a few days into tiny 1&nbsp;mm medusae, which are liberated and swim free from the parent hydroid colony.

== Distribution and invasion ==

''Turritopsis'' is believed to have originated in the [[Pacific Ocean|Pacific]], but has spread all over the world through trans-[[Arctic Ocean|Arctic]] migrations, and has speciated into several populations that are not easy to distinguish [[Morphology (biology)|morphologically]], but whose species distinctions have recently been verified by a study and comparison of mitochondrial ribosomal gene sequences.<ref name=mig>{{cite journal|last=Miglietta|first=M. P.|author2=S. Piraino |author3=S. Kubota |author4=P. Schuchert|date=November 2006|title=Species in the genus ''Turritopsis'' (Cnidaria, Hydrozoa): a molecular evaluation|journal=Journal of Zoological Systematics and Evolutionary Research|volume=45|issue=1|pages=11–19|publication-date=February 2007|doi=10.1111/j.1439-0469.2006.00379.x|doi-access=free}}</ref><ref name="telegraph-09">{{cite web|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/earth/wildlife/4357829/Immortal-jellyfish-swarming-across-the-world.html|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090130115250/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/earth/wildlife/4357829/Immortal-jellyfish-swarming-across-the-world.html|archive-date=January 30, 2009|title='Immortal' jellyfish swarming across the world |date=January 27, 2009|publisher=[[Telegraph Media Group]]|access-date=2010-06-16}}</ref> ''Turritopsis'' are found in temperate to tropical regions in all of the world's oceans.<ref name="TimesOnline26012009">{{cite news|url=http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/science/article5594539.ece|title=Turritopsis nutricula: the world's only 'immortal' creature|last=Mintowt-Czyz |first=Lech|date=26 January 2009|publisher=[[Times Online]]|access-date=2009-03-22|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090203063422/http://www.timesonline.co.uk/tol/news/uk/science/article5594539.ece|archive-date=February 3, 2009}}</ref> ''Turritopsis'' is believed to be spreading across the world through [[Ballast water discharge and the environment|ballast water discharge]].<ref name="TimesOnline26012009" /> Unlike other species invasions which caused serious economic and ecological consequences, ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii's'' invasion around the world was unnoticed due to their tiny size and innocuity.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Govindarajan|first1=Annette F.|last2=Carman|first2=Mary R.|date=2016-02-01|title=Possible cryptic invasion of the Western Pacific toxic population of the hydromedusa Gonionemus vertens (Cnidaria: Hydrozoa) in the Northwestern Atlantic Ocean|journal=Biological Invasions|language=en|volume=18|issue=2|pages=463–469|doi=10.1007/s10530-015-1019-8|bibcode=2016BiInv..18..463G |s2cid=17635921|issn=1387-3547}}</ref> "We are looking at a worldwide silent invasion", said [[Smithsonian Tropical Research Institute|Smithsonian Tropical Marine Institute]] scientist Maria Miglietta.<ref name="telegraph-09"/> ''Turritopsis'' are able to withstand extreme conditions, such as lack of food and harsh environments which would normally be fatal due to their ability to regress into a cluster of cells, possibly being carried by water currents. It is in this way that this species has invaded many environments by being so ecologically efficient and stronger than competing species.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Miglietta |first1=Maria Pia |last2=Lessios |first2=Harilaos A. |date=April 2009 |title=A silent invasion |url=https://link.springer.com/10.1007/s10530-008-9296-0 |journal=Biological Invasions |language=en |volume=11 |issue=4 |pages=825–834 |doi=10.1007/s10530-008-9296-0 |issn=1387-3547|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== Life cycle ==

The eggs develop in [[gonad]]s of female medusae, which are located in the walls of the manubrium (stomach). Mature eggs are presumably spawned and fertilized in the sea by sperm produced and released by male medusae, as is the case for most [[Hydrozoa|hydromedusae]]. However, the related species ''Turritopsis rubra'' seems to retain fertilized eggs until the planula stage.<ref name="schuchert-10">{{cite web|url=http://www.ville-ge.ch/mhng/hydrozoa/antho/turritopsis-rubra.htm |title=Turritopsis rubra |last=Schuchert |first=Peter |access-date=23 January 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090912180002/http://www.ville-ge.ch/mhng/hydrozoa/antho/turritopsis-rubra.htm |archive-date=September 12, 2009 }}</ref> Fertilized eggs develop into [[planula]] larvae, which settle onto the sea floor (or even the rich marine communities that live on floating docks), and develop into polyp colonies ([[hydroids]]). The hydroids bud new jellyfish, which are released at about one millimetre in size and then grow and feed in the plankton, becoming sexually mature after a few weeks (the exact duration depends on the ocean temperature; at {{convert|20|C|F}} it is 25 to 30 days and at {{convert|22|C|F}} it is 18 to 22 days).<ref name="piraino-96"/> Medusae of ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' are able to survive between {{convert|14 and 25|C|F}}.<ref name="piraino-96" /><ref name=":1" />

=== Biological immortality ===
{{See also|#Genomic analysis}}
Most jellyfish species have a relatively fixed lifespan, which varies by species from hours to many months (long-lived mature jellyfish spawn every day or night; the time is also fairly fixed and species-specific).<ref>{{cite journal|last=Mills|first=C. E.|year=1983|title=Vertical migration and diel activity patterns of hydromedusae: studies in a large tank|journal=Journal of Plankton Research|volume=5|pages=619–635|doi=10.1093/plankt/5.5.619|issue=5}}</ref> The medusa of ''Turritopsis dohrnii'' is the only form known to have the ability to return to a polyp state, by a specific transdifferentiation process that requires the presence of certain cell types (tissue from both the jellyfish bell surface and the circulatory canal system).<ref>{{cite web|url = https://www.zmescience.com/science/biology/meet-the-worlds-only-immortal-animal/|title = Meet the world's only immortal animal|date = December 5, 2008|access-date = January 10, 2015|website = ZME Science|last = Mihai|first = Andrei}}</ref>

Experiments have revealed that all stages of the medusae, from newly released to fully mature individuals, can transform back into polyps under the conditions of starvation, sudden temperature change, reduction of salinity, and artificial damage of the bell with forceps or scissors.<ref name="piraino-96" /> The transforming medusa is characterized first by deterioration of the bell, mesoglea, and tentacles. All immature medusa (with 12 tentacles at most) then turn into a [[Microbial cyst|cyst]]-like stage and proceed to transform into [[stolon]]s and polyps. However, about 20–40% of mature medusa instead proceed to the stolons and polyps stage without passing the cyst-like stage. Polyps are formed 2 days after stolons have developed and fed. Polyps further multiply by growing additional stolons, branches, and then polyps to form colonial [[hydroids]]. In one experiment, they would eventually transform into stolons and polyps and begin their lives once again, even in the absence of environmental changes or injury.<ref name="piraino-96" />

This ability to reverse the biotic cycle (in response to adverse conditions) is unique in the animal kingdom. It allows the jellyfish to bypass death, rendering ''Turritopsis dohrnii'' potentially biologically immortal. The process has not been observed in their natural habitat, in part because the process is quite rapid and because field observations at the right moment are unlikely.<ref name="piraino-96" /> Regardless, most individual medusae are likely to fall victim to the general hazards of life as [[mesoplankton]], including being eaten by predators or succumbing to disease.

The species possesses unique mechanisms related to [[telomere]] maintenance, which play a significant role in its regenerative abilities. ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' maintains telomere length through specific cellular processes during its life cycle reversal, effectively resetting cellular aging.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Pascual-Torner |first1=M. |last2=Carrero |first2=D. |last3=Pérez-Silva |first3=J. G. |last4=López-Otín |first4=C. |year=2022 |title=Comparative genomics of mortal and immortal cnidarians unveils novel keys behind rejuvenation |journal=[[Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences]] |volume=119 |issue=36 |article-number=e2118763119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2118763119|pmid=36037356 |doi-access=free |pmc=9459311 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11918763P }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Miglietta |first1=M. P. |last2=Schuchert |first2=P. |last3=Cunningham |first3=C. W. |year=2017 |title=Complete mitochondrial genome and evolutionary analysis of ''Turritopsis dohrnii'', the "immortal" jellyfish with a reversible life-cycle |journal=[[Molecular Phylogenetics and Evolution]] |volume=107 |pages=232–238 |doi=10.1016/j.ympev.2016.11.007|pmid=27845203 |bibcode=2017MolPE.107..232L |doi-access=free }}</ref>

The species' cell development method of transdifferentiation has inspired scientists to find a way to use this process to renew damaged or dead tissue in [[human]]s.<ref name=":0">{{cite web|last=Dimberu|first=Peniel M.|title=Immortal Jellyfish Provides Clues for Regenerative Medicine|url=https://singularityhub.com/2011/04/25/immortal-jellyfish-provides-clues-for-regenerative-medicine/|publisher=Singularity Hub|access-date=26 October 2011|date=2011-04-25}}</ref>

It was first discovered in 1992 that the jellyfish is immortal, after an Italian scientist accidentally left them to starve over a weekend.<ref>{{cite web |last=Zimmer |first=Katarina |date= 19 October 2026 |title=The immortal jellyfish can beat ageing. It could inspire treatments for diseases of old age |url=https://www.bbc.com/future/article/20261016-the-rejuvenating-secrets-of-the-immortal-jellyfish |website=BBC |location= |publisher= |access-date=2 November 2026}}</ref>

== Ecology ==

=== Diet ===
''Turritopsis dohrnii'' are a carnivorous species that commonly feed on [[zooplankton]].<ref name="NEMESIS Database Species Summary">{{Cite web|title=NEMESIS Database Species Summary|url=https://invasions.si.edu/nemesis/browseDB/SpeciesSummary.jsp?TSN=-502#:~:text=Description:,up%20to%2035%20mm%20high.&text=The%20pear-shaped%20medusa%20buds,stems%20from%20below%20the%20hydranths.|access-date=2020-10-21|website=invasions.si.edu}}</ref> Their diet mainly consists of plankton, fish eggs and small [[Mollusca|mollusks]]. ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' ingests food and excretes waste through the mouth. ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' hunts by using its tentacles as it drifts through the water.  Its tentacles, which contain stinging cells called nematocysts, spread and sting its prey.<ref name="britannica.com">{{Cite web|title=jellyfish {{!}} Characteristics, Habitat, Diet, Anatomy, & Facts|url=https://www.britannica.com/animal/jellyfish|access-date=2020-10-21|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|language=en}}</ref> The tentacles can then flex to direct its prey to the mouth. ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'', like other jellyfish, may use its bell to catch its prey.  ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii''<nowiki/>'s bell will expand, sucking in water, as it propels itself to swim.  This expansion of the bell brings potential prey in closer reach of the tentacles.<ref name="britannica.com"/>

=== Predation ===
''Turritopsis dohrnii'', like other jellyfish, are preyed on most commonly by other jellyfish.  Other predators of ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' include [[Sea anemone|sea anemones]], [[tuna]], [[Shark|sharks]], [[swordfish]], [[Sea turtle|sea turtles]], and [[Penguin|penguins]].<ref name="britannica.com"/> Many species prey on ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' and other jellyfish due to their simple composition. They are only approximately 5% non-aqueous matter, and the remaining part is composed of water.<ref name="US Department of Commerce">{{Cite web|publisher=National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration|title=What are jellyfish made of?|url=https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/jellyfish.html#:~:text=They%20are%20composed%20of%20three,and%20respond%20to%20other%20stimuli.|access-date=2020-10-21|website=oceanservice.noaa.gov|language=EN-US}}</ref>  They are composed of [[Jellyfish#Anatomy|three layers]] - an outer layer (the epidermis), a middle layer (mesoglea - a thick, jelly-like substance), and an inner layer (gastrodermis).<ref name="US Department of Commerce"/>

=== Habitat ===
''Turritopsis dohrnii'' was first discovered in the [[Mediterranean Sea]] but has since been found worldwide.<ref>{{Cite web|date=May 4, 2015|title=The Immortal Jellyfish|url=https://www.amnh.org/explore/news-blogs/on-exhibit-posts/the-immortal-jellyfish#:~:text=Only%20one%20animal%20is%20known,Limits%3A%20Stories%20of%20Amazing%20Species.|access-date=October 21, 2020|website=American Museum of Natural History}}</ref> ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' is generally found living in temperate to tropical waters. They can be found in [[Marina|marinas]] or [[Dock|docks]], on vessel [[Hull (watercraft)|hulls]], and on the [[ocean floor]]. They typically live in a [[salinity]] range of [[polyhaline]] (18–30 PSU) and euhaline (30–40 PSU).<ref name="NEMESIS Database Species Summary"/>

== Genomic analysis ==
Genomic analyses such as [[sequence analysis]] on [[Messenger RNA|mRNA]] or [[Mitochondrion|mitochondria]] DNA have been employed to investigate its lifecycle. mRNA analysis of each life stage showed that a stage-specific gene in the medusae stage is expressed tenfold more than in other stages. This gene is relative to a [[Wnt signaling pathway|Wnt signal]] that can induce a regeneration process upon injury.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Logan|first1=Catriona Y.|last2=Nusse|first2=Roel|date=2004-10-08|title=The wnt signaling pathway in development and disease|journal=Annual Review of Cell and Developmental Biology|volume=20|issue=1|pages=781–810|doi=10.1146/annurev.cellbio.20.010403.113126|pmid=15473860|issn=1081-0706|citeseerx=10.1.1.322.311}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Hasegawa|first1=Yoshinori|last2=Watanabe|first2=Takashi|last3=Takazawa|first3=Masaki|last4=Ohara|first4=Osamu|last5=Kubota|first5=Shin|date=2016-08-01|title=De Novo Assembly of the Transcriptome of Turritopsis, a Jellyfish that Repeatedly Rejuvenates|journal=Zoological Science|volume=33|issue=4|pages=366–371|doi=10.2108/zs150186|pmid=27498796|issn=0289-0003|hdl=2433/216317|s2cid=34161600|hdl-access=free}}</ref>

Analysis of [[Nucleic acid sequence|nucleotide sequence]] homologs and protein homologs identified ''[[Nemopsis bachei]]'' as the species' closest relative. None of the closely related species display biological immortality.<ref>{{Cite journal|last1=Devarapalli|first1=Pratap|title=The conserved mitochondrial gene distribution in relatives of Turritopsis nutricula, an immortal jellyfish|journal=Bioinformation|volume=10|issue=9|pages=586–591|doi=10.6026/97320630010586|pmid=25352727|pmc=4209368|year=2014}}</ref>

In 2022, a study reported [[Longevity#Non-human biological longevity|the key molecular mechanisms]] of [[rejuvenation]] they found in a [[Comparative genomics|comparison of the newly presented genomes]] of this biologically immortal jellyfish and a similar but non-rejuvenating jellyfish, involving e.g. [[DNA replication]] and [[DNA repair|repair]], and stem cell renewal.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Greenwood |first1=Veronique |title=This Jellyfish Can Live Forever. Its Genes May Tell Us How. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/06/science/immortal-jellyfish-gene-protein.html |access-date=22 September 2022 |work=The New York Times |date=6 September 2022}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Pascual-Torner |first1=Maria |last2=Carrero |first2=Dido |last3=Pérez-Silva |first3=José G. |last4=Álvarez-Puente |first4=Diana |last5=Roiz-Valle |first5=David |last6=Bretones |first6=Gabriel |last7=Rodríguez |first7=David |last8=Maeso |first8=Daniel |last9=Mateo-González |first9=Elena |last10=Español |first10=Yaiza |last11=Mariño |first11=Guillermo |last12=Acuña |first12=José Luis |last13=Quesada |first13=Víctor |last14=López-Otín |first14=Carlos |title=Comparative genomics of mortal and immortal cnidarians unveils novel keys behind rejuvenation |journal=Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences |date=6 September 2022 |volume=119 |issue=36 |article-number=e2118763119 |doi=10.1073/pnas.2118763119 |doi-access=free |pmid=36037356 |pmc=9459311 |bibcode=2022PNAS..11918763P |language=en |issn=0027-8424}}</ref>

==Culturing==

Keeping ''T.&nbsp;dohrnii'' in captivity is quite difficult. Currently, only one scientist, Shin Kubota from [[Kyoto University]], has managed to sustain a group of these jellyfish for a prolonged period of time. The plankton must be inspected daily to ensure that they have properly digested the [[Brine shrimp|Artemia cysts]] they are being fed.<ref name=nytm/>
Kubota reported that during a two-year period, his colony rebirthed itself 11 times.<ref>{{cite journal|author=S Kubota|title=Repeating rejuvenation in Turritopsis, an immortal hydrozoan (Cnidaria, Hydrozoa)|journal=Biogeography|issn=1345-0662|year=2011|volume=13|pages=101–103|url=http://www.benikurage.com/jp/2011biogeography.pdf|url-status=usurped|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141029093746/http://www.benikurage.com/jp/2011biogeography.pdf|archive-date=2014-10-29}}</ref> Kubota regularly appears on Japanese television to talk about his immortal jellyfish and has recorded several songs about them, often singing them at the end of his conference presentations.<ref name=nytm>{{cite news |last=Rich|first=Nathaniel|title=Can a jellyfish unlock the secret of immortality? |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2012/12/02/magazine/can-a-jellyfish-unlock-the-secret-of-immortality.html?src=me&ref=general|work=[[The New York Times Magazine]] |date=November 28, 2012|access-date=October 22, 2017}}</ref><ref>{{Citation |title=Shin Kubota sings a song of jellyfish | date=18 August 2015 |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=o3Q7ETDlr3s |language=en |access-date=2023-01-06}}</ref>

== In popular culture ==
Turritopsis Dohrnii Teo En Ming, a [[perennial candidate]] in [[Singapore]]an elections,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ching Shi Jie |date=2023-06-30 |title=PE2023: IT consultant says he's running for president, wants to be 'SAF commander-in-chief' if he wins |url=https://www.asiaone.com/singapore/pe-2023-it-consultant-says-hes-running-president-wants-be-saf-commander-chief-if-he-wins-teo-en-ming |access-date=2025-08-19 |website=AsiaOne}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Yustika |date=2025-04-22 |title=Four independents declare intention to contest in GE2025 – but will they qualify? |url=https://www.theonlinecitizen.com/2025/04/22/four-independents-declare-intention-to-contest-in-ge2025-but-will-they-qualify/ |access-date=2025-08-19 |website=The Online Citizen |language=en-GB}}</ref> legally changed his name after the jellyfish.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Wong |first=Julian |date=2018-04-14 |title=Teo En Ming: Aspiring Actor and 40-Year Old Virgin |url=https://www.ricemedia.co/culture-people-teo-en-ming-aspiring-actor-40-year-old-virgin/ |access-date=2025-08-19 |website=RICE |language=en-GB}}</ref>

